WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.210
OT topologies connect physical processes with control, supervision, engineering, operations,

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.444
and enterprise functions.

00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:10.787
At the field level, sensors measure process conditions and actuators create physical action.

00:00:10.787 --> 00:00:16.131
Controllers such as PLCs and RTUs read inputs, execute logic, and update outputs.

00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:20.652
Human-machine interfaces give operators visibility into status, alarms, trends, and commands.

00:00:20.652 --> 00:00:24.763
SCADA systems coordinate monitoring and supervisory control across distributed assets.

00:00:24.763 --> 00:00:28.873
Distributed control systems support coordinated control within plants or processes.

00:00:28.873 --> 00:00:31.339
Engineering workstations configure controllers and applications,

00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:33.806
while historians store time-series operational data.

00:00:33.806 --> 00:00:38.738
These systems also connect to site services and enterprise applications for scheduling,

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.616
reporting, maintenance, identity, remote support, and analytics.

00:00:41.616 --> 00:00:46.137
Purdue-style layers can provide a useful mental model for these functions,

00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:51.070
but the actual environment may include cloud platforms, wireless devices, vendor appliances,

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.769
remote sites, and legacy links that cross traditional boundaries.

00:00:54.769 --> 00:00:57.646
Topology matters because communication paths shape risk.

00:00:57.646 --> 00:01:02.579
A reporting flow from a historian to an enterprise application has different

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.689
consequences from an engineering path that can modify controller logic.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:11.622
A remote support connection may bypass expected boundaries if it uses a

00:01:11.622 --> 00:01:13.677
separate cellular or vendor-managed service.

00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:19.021
Accurate diagrams should show assets, trust boundaries, data and control flows, remote access,

00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:21.076
dependencies, redundancy, and external connections.

00:01:21.076 --> 00:01:24.364
This information supports segmentation, monitoring placement, vulnerability analysis,

00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:25.598
and incident containment.

00:01:25.598 --> 00:01:26.420
During response,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.352
teams need to know which functions can continue locally and what may

00:01:31.352 --> 00:01:34.230
be lost when a connection is isolated.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.340
Security architecture begins with understanding how the operation actually communicates.
